# naked_fn_attribute.tcyr — v6.2.27
#
# Gates the #naked fn-attribute (bare-metal interrupt-handler emit: NO
# prologue/epilogue/frame). Covers three things the v6.2.27 review surfaced:
#  1. Annotation-fork-desync: token 133 (#naked; 128 was already f64v_dot) must be
#     consumed in pass-1 + pass-2 of ALL forks, else a #naked fn drops into a
#     fork's catchall → "unexpected enum" on that target. check.sh compiles this
#     corpus on x86 + cross-OS (ecb/cass/pi), so a missed fork fails the build there.
#  2. The DCE-stub leak (review P1): a NON-underscore, unreferenced #naked fn that
#     gets DCE-stubbed must clear _naked_pending so the NEXT live fn (here, main)
#     is still framed — else main is emitted frameless and faults on rbp-relative
#     arg-home stores against rbp=0 (SIGSEGV). `isr_probe` (no leading underscore)
#     is DCE-eligible, so it exercises the stub path; main returning 0 below proves
#     it kept its prologue.
#  3. The safe naked contract (review P2): a #naked fn is paramless (an ISR gets no
#     args) and contains no `return` (its body must self-terminate in asm — cyrius
#     has no inline asm yet, so the body is empty here; #naked is the prologue-skip
#     building block, full ISRs await inline-asm). A param or `return` is a hard
#     compile error (verified manually at the cut).

include "lib/assert.cyr"

#naked fn isr_probe() {
}

fn main(): i64 {
    assert_eq(1, 1, "main keeps its frame after a DCE-stubbed non-underscore #naked fn (review P1)");
    var r = assert_summary();
    return r;
}
